; robotics composes from 3D spatial structure and physics kinematics
; the workspace IS aayaama-dvaya — the 2D tala for a planar arm
; each link IS a rekha (directed segment) in that tala
; each joint IS a sandhi — where two rekha meet with a kona (angle) state
; the kinematic chain IS the scene-graph — transforms compose from base to tip
; angular velocity IS kramanusara of kona wrt time (same as physics, different quantity)
; spatial structure — the workspace
; structural elements
; kinematic quantities (from physics, reused as-is)
; derivative structure
; kinematic chain bridges 3D and physics perspectives
; the arm IS a kinematic-chain: scene-graph compose = FK, invert = IK
; bone world-space position = end-effector bindu in aayaama-dvaya
; motion plan = keyframe trajectory in joint-space (kona space)
; computation: inverse kinematics, forward kinematics, motion planning

(kosha domain-robotics
  (yukta domain-3d domain-physics domain-math akasham bindu rekha armature bone scene-graph kona angular-velocity time velocity displacement kramanusara apeksha kinematic-chain world-space)
  (sthita aayaama-dvaya tala)
  (siddha yantra upakarana))
